changelog: |
  0.2.0 — the kit becomes deliverable, and its contract becomes testable.

  * THREE LAYERS IN ONE FILE. fleet-feedback.js is now capture buffer +
    `FleetFeedbackCore` (the issue contract as pure functions, no DOM) +
    `<fleet-feedback>` (the default dialog, rendered only where the tag is).
    A host with its own UI — the zer0-mistakes theme's Bootstrap modal — loads
    the file for the first two layers and never writes the tag, so both widgets
    file byte-identical issues instead of two divergent ones.
  * capture.js: the buffer alone, for <head>. Byte-identical to the block
    inside fleet-feedback.js; tests/contract.test.mjs fails if they diverge.
    Without it, capture starts after page load and the dialog says so rather
    than implying it saw errors it structurally could not.
  * 25 contract tests (`npm test`, node:test, no dependencies): section order,
    marker, labels, assignment, escaping, the 7000-char trim ladder, redaction,
    and taxonomy parity across the JS fallback, feedback_types.yml, and the
    no-JS issue form. Two real defects fell out of writing them — the Markdown
    cell escaper doubled backslashes, and the credential scrubber stopped at
    the scheme word, leaving "Authorization: Bearer <token>" in the buffer.
  * Privacy: every captured line is redacted (bearer/API keys, JWTs, GitHub
    tokens, email addresses) BEFORE it enters the ring, so a secret can never
    be previewed, copied, or filed.
  * `breadcrumb(level, message)` so an app's fetch wrapper can put the API call
    that broke into the report, not just the exception it surfaced as.
  * `mode="postmessage"` for sandboxed hosts (VS Code webviews) with no
    window.open of their own.
  * Adapters: FeedbackButton.tsx now compiles under React 19 (the global JSX
    namespace it augmented no longer exists) and under Next (it read Vite's
    `import.meta.env`); new nextjs.tsx installs capture with
    `strategy="beforeInteractive"`; jekyll.html ships the taxonomy inline
    instead of pointing at a /feedback-types.json nothing generated.
  * Wired into tools/fanout.sh (`--kit feedback`) and drift check (i), so a
    vendored copy that drifts from the hub is now visible.
notes: |
  - Vendor fleet-feedback.js + capture.js into the repo (assets/js, public/,
    static/js); never hot-link the hub.
  - Every label in feedback_types.yml MUST exist in the target repo:
    `gh label list`. GitHub silently drops unknown labels from prefilled URLs —
    no error, no warning. The fan-out creates them.
  - EDIT THE CAPTURE BLOCK IN capture.js, then re-splice it into
    fleet-feedback.js (the parity test tells you when you forgot).
  - Snapshot the outgoing fleet-feedback.js into archive/ BEFORE editing it, or
    every deployed copy reads as hand-modified and --upgrade stops reaching the
    fleet.
